WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.050
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about the AWS Client VPN.</v>

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.510
So this allows you to connect from your computer

3
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:10.050
using OpenVPN to your private network in AWS

4
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.400
and on-premises.

5
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.233
So how does that work?

6
00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:14.850
Well, you install the software on your computer

7
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.450
and then you're going to directly use a connection

8
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.230
over the public internet into your VPC,

9
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.110
but now you can access your EC2 instances

10
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.300
using a private IP.

11
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.280
And on top of it, if you've enabled, for example,

12
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.400
a site-to-site VPC, then it's possible for you to access

13
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.520
your corporate data center through the site-to-site VPC

14
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.360
through the client VPN

15
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.000
and therefore making your computer access

16
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.450
your internal resources within your corporate data center.

17
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.030
So just to give you a bunch of possible

18
00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.100
network architecture this enables.

19
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.080
So say you have VPC peering,

20
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.290
well, all you need to do is to create one

21
00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.290
Client VPN connection into one of these VPCs.

22
00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.030
And then thanks to VPC peering,

23
00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.140
you can access resources in the VPC-B through the VPC-A

24
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.500
through your Client VPN endpoint.

25
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.670
Same for on-premises.

26
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.160
So again, if you've enabled site-to-site VPN

27
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.330
or Direct Connect, then getting a Client VPN endpoint

28
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:16.530
in VPC-A allows you to access any resources on-premises.

29
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.680
So that means that the traffic has to go through AWS

30
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.530
to get to your corporate data center.

31
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.590
You can also get internet access from Client VPN,

32
00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.140
so this gives you one endpoint for everything.

33
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.090
So say you have a VPC

34
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.030
and you've enabled it with two public subnets

35
00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.500
and an internet gateway,

36
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.930
well, you create a Client VPN endpoint

37
00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.790
and this endpoint is going to be connected

38
00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.860
to the two public subnets.

39
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.540
That means that thanks to this,

40
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.160
there is a route to the internet

41
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.660
through the internet gateway.

42
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:46.620
So that means that users

43
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.070
can both access your resources on your VPC

44
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.820
thanks to the VPN connection, but also access the internet

45
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.613
through your VPC to the internet gateway.

46
00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.090
You can also do the same if you have, for example,

47
00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.070
private subnets and public subnets,

48
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.560
but this time, of course, you need to use a NAT device,

49
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.360
so a NAT gateway, for example,

50
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.213
and the architecture is going to be quite similar.

51
00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.400
What if you have a Transit Gateway?

52
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.450
Well, everything is possible.

53
00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.420
So we've seen that we can do VPC peering

54
00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.080
to access VPC-A and VPC-B through a Client VPN,

55
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.810
but also if you had set up other VPCs

56
00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.360
and they're connected through a Transit Gateway,

57
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.800
it is totally possible for you

58
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.490
to use your Client VPN connection to access VPC-B and C

59
00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.590
through the Transit Gateway.

60
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.960
And of course, Transit Gateway can also connect

61
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.550
to corporate data centers.

62
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.530
So if you have a Direct Connect connection

63
00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.420
or a site-to-site VPN connection,

64
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.060
it's definitely possible to access everything

65
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.420
through Client VPN through Transit Gateway and so on.

66
00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.060
So that's it, hopefully that makes sense for Client VPN.

67
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.640
It just enables you to get a private connection into AWS,

68
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.520
and therefore, any network architecture on AWS

69
00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.170
should work with Client VPN.

70
00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.070
So I hope you liked it

71
00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.020
and I will see you in the next lecture.

